      ":question/id": "7d1aee54-0bcd-54dc-9a0d-876fc00bc6cf",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny\\prime=\\frac{5 \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)}}{4}\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)=\\frac{\\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)}}{4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f\\prime(x)=\\frac{5 \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)}}{4}.$\n- **II.** Substituting $y=f(x)$ and $y\\prime=f\\prime(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement.\n- **III.** The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in an interval where the function is defined and the equation is valid.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=\\frac{\\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)}}{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\prime(x)&=\\left(\\frac{\\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)}}{4}\\right)\\prime \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4\\cos(4x) \\\\ &=\\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)} \\\\ &\\not\\equiv \\frac{5 \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Substituting $y=\\frac{\\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)}}{4}$ and $y\\prime=\\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)}$ into the original equation gives expressions that are not identical:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\prime &\\overset{?}{=}\\frac{5 \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)}}{4} \\\\ \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)} &\\not\\equiv \\frac{5 \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the function $y=\\frac{\\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)}}{4}$ is not a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "cf18c47a-b240-5f7c-9957-eba882de0e4c",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
