      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny\\prime=\\frac{5 \\cos{\\left(5 x \\right)}}{2}\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)=\\frac{\\sin{\\left(5 x \\right)}}{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f\\prime(x)=\\frac{5 \\cos{\\left(5 x \\right)}}{2}.$\n- **II.** Substituting $y=f(x)$ and $y\\prime=f\\prime(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement.\n- **III.** The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in an interval where the function is defined and the equation is valid.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x)=\\frac{\\sin{\\left(5 x \\right)}}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\prime(x)&=\\left(\\frac{\\sin{\\left(5 x \\right)}}{2}\\right)\\prime \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cos(5x) \\\\ &=\\frac{5 \\cos{\\left(5 x \\right)}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=\\frac{\\sin{\\left(5 x \\right)}}{2}$ and $y\\prime=\\frac{5 \\cos{\\left(5 x \\right)}}{2}$ into the original equation gives an identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\prime &\\overset{?}{=}\\frac{5 \\cos{\\left(5 x \\right)}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{5 \\cos{\\left(5 x \\right)}}{2} &=\\frac{5 \\cos{\\left(5 x \\right)}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=\\frac{\\sin{\\left(5 x \\right)}}{2}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"",
